  "narrative": "No assessments were due, and no new validation was delivered; the validation budget is exhausted. Overall, the supplied dataset supports a small number of large, clinically meaningful observational associations between measured predictors and CRISPR dependency scores. These associations are robust across many lineage, library, screen-quality, growth, serum, aneuploidy, mutation-burden, and co-alteration strata. By contrast, most matched-driver, lineage-baseline, MYC-biosynthetic, and effect-modification hypotheses are unsupported or remain too uncertain. All conclusions are observational associations in this supplied dataset; they are not causal effects and should not be interpreted as externally generalizable without replication.\n\nSupported findings, with practical relevance judged against approximately 10% of the absolute mean dependency score:\n1. SMARCA4 loss is associated with markedly stronger dependency_SMARCA2. Discovery signed estimate +0.784, CI +0.765 to +0.803; validation signed estimate +0.786, CI +0.757 to +0.815. In raw dependency units, SMARCA4-loss cells have SMARCA2 scores about 0.79 units more negative than SMARCA4-intact cells, far above the approximate 0.042 SMARCA2 benchmark.\n2. MSI-high status is associated with markedly stronger dependency_WRN. Discovery +0.790, CI +0.760 to +0.819; validation +0.805, CI +0.758 to +0.851. This corresponds to WRN scores about 0.80 units more negative in MSI-high cells, far above the approximate 0.030 WRN benchmark.\n3. KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker dependency_NFE2L2. Discovery +0.796, CI +0.776 to +0.816; validation +0.829, CI +0.800 to +0.857. Raw scores are about 0.83 units less negative in KEAP1-loss cells, indicating much weaker NFE2L2 dependency. The NFE2L2 mean is near zero, but the absolute effect is very large and precisely estimated.\n4. PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency_STAT3. Discovery +0.267, CI +0.215 to +0.319; validation +0.307, CI +0.228 to +0.387. Raw scores are about 0.31 units more negative in PTEN-loss cells, roughly 95 to 110% of the absolute mean STAT3 scale and well above the approximate 0.028 benchmark.\n5. TP53 loss is associated with weaker dependency_USP7. Discovery +0.396, CI +0.325 to +0.467; validation +0.382, CI +0.280 to +0.485. Raw scores are about 0.38 units less negative in TP53-loss cells, far above the approximate 0.018 USP7 benchmark.\n6. CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells is associated with stronger dependency_MDM2. Discovery +0.119, CI +0.079 to +0.158; validation +0.112, CI +0.053 to +0.171. Raw scores are about 0.11 units more negative, about 40 to 50% of the absolute mean MDM2 scale and above the approximate 0.027 benchmark. Additional stratum support is strong, although the central accepted claim is conditional on TP53-intact status.\n\nThese supported effects are not merely isolated main contrasts. SMARCA4 loss maintains a very large SMARCA2 effect in Avana and non-Avana libraries, high and lower Cas9 activity, high aneuploidy, high mutation burden, adenocarcinoma, ARID1A-loss and ARID1A-intact cells, TP53-loss cells, and growth-rate strata. MSI-high maintains a very large WRN effect across lineage subtypes, library, Cas9, serum, growth, screen quality, and BRCA2-loss status. KEAP1 loss maintains a very large NFE2L2 effect across lineage, library, serum, growth, screen quality, STK11, TP53, MYC, and ARID1A strata. PTEN loss maintains a strong STAT3 effect across lineage, library, Cas9, TP53, STK11, PIK3CA, growth, hypoxia, EMT, and IFN-gamma strata. TP53 loss maintains a strong USP7 effect across lineage, library, Cas9, MYC, CDKN2A, KEAP1, and growth strata. CDKN2A loss in TP53-intact cells is consistent across lineage, library, Cas9, growth, screen quality, MTAP, and other co-alteration strata.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings include the following:\n- Matched-driver dependency is not supported for most canonical genotype-outcome pairs tested: BRAF V600E did not show meaningful stronger BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification did not show a clinically meaningful main EGFR effect, ERBB2 amplification did not show a meaningful ERBB2 effect, and KRAS mutation did not show a consistent KRAS effect. ARID1A loss did not show the hypothesized SMARCA2 association.\n- MYC amplification was not associated with meaningful POLR2A, RPL11, USP7, STAT3, or DHX9 effects. Its associations with KIF18A and WRN were weak or borderline; the WRN reversal signal is small in lower bound and closed as low-priority unresolved.\n- TP53 loss was not meaningfully associated with MDM2 dependency overall, although the separate conditional CDKN2A-loss effect among TP53-intact cells was strongly supported.\n- New PTEN-loss mechanistic probes were weak: PTEN loss was not associated with meaningful POLR2A or RPL11 dependency.\n- Lineage subtype alone was not a robust predictor for the tested dependencies. Adenocarcinoma-versus-squamous contrasts for ERBB2, NFE2L2, STAT3, SMARCA2, and WRN were small, null, or inconsistent.\n- Most interaction hypotheses are unsupported. There is no robust evidence for effect modification by lineage, library, Cas9, many co-mutations, IFN/hypoxia/EMT, TP53, MYC, or research markers. The PTEN-by-library STAT3 interaction showed a discovery signal but an opposite near-null validation signal and remains unresolved. The PTEN-by-TP53 interaction remains unsupported in the registered direction and unresolved for the reversal claim.\n- Research markers D, E, and F remain untested in the available evidence for dependency_TMED10, so no conclusion can be drawn.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty is concentrated in low-precision subgroup and effect-modification claims rather than in the main accepted effects:\n- The most informative unresolved discovery is H90: EGFR amplification among KRAS-mutant cells has a signed estimate +0.053, CI +0.0035 to +0.103. The point estimate exceeds the approximate 0.025 EGFR benchmark, but the lower confidence bound is below that benchmark and there is no independent validation. Therefore it is scientifically interesting but not accepted.\n- MYC amplification versus weaker WRN and RB1 loss versus weaker DHX9 produce small opposite-direction signals with lower bounds below practical thresholds; these are closed as low-priority unresolved observations.\n- KRAS effects within STK11-loss contexts and TP53-intact STK11-loss contexts remain unresolved and imprecise.\n- Some robustness strata for accepted effects remain untested, including female-specific strata for the main accepted associations and a direct squamous SMARCA4-loss stratum. These do not weaken current conclusions but would improve population-specific robustness assessment.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is targeted follow-up of the EGFR amplification effect among KRAS-mutant cells. If independent validation were available, H90 would be the best candidate. Since validation budget is exhausted, the next discovery analyses should test whether this signal survives in high-quality technical subsets, particularly KRAS-mutant cells with high Cas9 activity or Avana screening, and should complete the registered female-stratum tests for the accepted main effects. These targeted analyses are more informative than further broad exploration of already-supported associations or additional sparse interaction hypotheses.\n\nThe final scientific conclusion is that this dataset provides strong internal evidence for six association patterns: SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, MSI-high/WRN, KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, PTEN-loss/STAT3, TP53-loss/USP7, and CDKN2A-loss-without-TP53-loss/MDM2. These are large, precisely estimated, validation-supported associations. The evidence does not support the broader hypothesis that matched driver alterations generally predict their own CRISPR dependency in this dataset, nor does it support most effect-modification hypotheses. The EGFR-amplified KRAS-mutant subgroup remains the principal unresolved lead.",
