  "narrative": "All returned estimates are signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive values support the registered claim. For practical importance I again used approximate 10% of the absolute mean dependency-score scale as a benchmark: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, STAT3 about 0.028, USP7 about 0.018, MDM2 about 0.027, EGFR about 0.025, and NFE2L2 about 0.009. H81 shows that the SMARCA4-loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency remains very large and precise in the squamous stratum: signed estimate +0.805, interval +0.762 to +0.848. This is far above the SMARCA2 benchmark and completes lineage robustness for that accepted association, so I accept and close H81. H197, H198, H199, H201, H203, and H208 provide female-stratum estimates for the six core accepted associations. All have large positive signed estimates with lower bounds far above their outcome benchmarks: SMARCA2 +0.792/+0.756, WRN +0.797/+0.744, STAT3 +0.237/+0.156, USP7 +0.421/+0.313, MDM2 +0.133/+0.068, and NFE2L2 +0.779/+0.746. These are not new biological mechanisms but robustness checks; they support the conclusion that the main dataset-only associations are not confined to male samples or a single demographic stratum. I accept and close all six. H218 tests the moderate CDKN2A-loss effect on MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact cells in the low-serum context; signed estimate +0.123 with lower bound +0.056 exceeds the MDM2 benchmark, so I accept and close it as a robustness stratum for the accepted conditional association. The EGFR-amplification/KRAS-mutant lead is less informative. The high-Cas9 subset H209 gives +0.022 but interval -0.046 to +0.091. The Avana subset H210 gives +0.047 but lower bound only -0.007. Avana plus high Cas9 H211 gives +0.027 with lower bound -0.047. The high screen-ROC subset H212 gives the largest point estimate, +0.092, but the lower bound is only +0.008 and the exposed cell has n=20, so it remains below the practical EGFR benchmark and is vulnerable to selection from multiple technical subsets. These checks do not resolve the lead; they show that the signal is not consistently precise enough for acceptance. I leave H209-H212 unresolved and close them. I also close H90 and H153 as unresolved because validation budget is exhausted and the registered technical refinements were not supportive enough. To avoid further fishing in fragile subsets, I propose a single focused effect-modification comparison: whether the EGFR-amplification effect on dependency_EGFR differs between KRAS-mutant and KRAS-intact cells. This tests contingency of the EGFR signal on KRAS context directly, rather than relying on the small KRAS-mutant/EGFR-amplified subset alone. All conclusions remain observational associations in this supplied dataset and do not establish causality or external generalizability.",
