  "narrative": "All nine newly delivered discovery comparisons have valid Welch cells with at least 20 observations in each required cell, and the estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis directions. The first six results are internal robustness tests of the six core dataset-only associations within male cells with adequate Cas9 activity. H292/R259 shows a very large SMARCA4-loss effect on SMARCA2 dependency, signed estimate +0.794, interval +0.764 to +0.824, far above the approximate practical benchmark for SMARCA2. H293/R260 shows a very large MSI-high effect on WRN dependency, signed estimate +0.784, interval +0.734 to +0.833, with an exposed cell size of 49 that is adequate but smaller; the effect remains enormous and decisive. H294/R261 supports the accepted weaker-dependency interpretation of KEAP1 loss for NFE2L2, signed estimate +0.798, interval +0.766 to +0.831. H295/R262 supports PTEN-loss associated stronger STAT3 dependency, signed estimate +0.242, interval +0.156 to +0.328, clearly above the STAT3 practical benchmark. H296/R263 supports TP53-loss associated weaker USP7 dependency, signed estimate +0.454, interval +0.337 to +0.572. H297/R264 supports CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells being associated with stronger MDM2 dependency, signed estimate +0.076, interval +0.014 to +0.137; the lower bound is slightly below the approximate MDM2 practical benchmark, so the stratum-specific magnitude is less certain, but the statistically significant point estimate is clinically relevant and consistent with the validated core CDKN2A/TP53-intact/MDM2 association. I therefore accept these as dataset-only evidence that the core associations are not confined to female cells or to low-Cas9 strata, while preserving all usual caution that these are associations rather than causal or externally generalizable claims. The next two comparisons test whether STK11 loss is associated with stronger USP7 dependency separately among TP53-intact and TP53-loss cells. H298/R265 gives a very small positive signed estimate, +0.006, interval -0.016 to +0.028, compatible with null or trivial effects. H299/R266 gives a negative signed estimate, -0.105, interval -0.297 to +0.087, not supporting the hypothesized stronger USP7-dependency direction and highly uncertain. I reject both claims as unsupported in the tested direction; this is not proof that STK11 loss has no USP7-related relationship whatsoever, but the present evidence does not support the registered hypothesis. H300/R267 tests BRCA2 loss with KIF18A dependency: signed estimate -0.014, interval -0.049 to +0.021. The result is non-significant, small, and not clinically meaningful in the hypothesized direction; I reject the registered KIF18A association claim. No validation request is made because the independent validation budget is exhausted. Multiplicity is a concern for all small subgroup follow-ups, but the accepted core associations have effects far above practical thresholds, whereas the rejected new claims do not show clinically meaningful support.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 22/25 appraisal update: H292-H297 accepted; they show that the six core dataset-only associations are also present in male cells with adequate Cas9 activity. These remain associations in measured states, not causal vulnerability claims or external generalizability evidence. H297 is accepted but is the least precise of the six, with lower bound slightly below the approximate MDM2 practical benchmark. H298-H300 are rejected as unsupported: no evidence that STK11 loss is associated with stronger USP7 dependency among TP53-intact cells, no evidence in TP53-loss cells, and no evidence that BRCA2 loss is associated with stronger KIF18A dependency. Independent validation budget is exhausted, so no validation request is possible. The controller-maintained accepted core set should be interpreted as: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency; TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; and CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells with stronger MDM2 dependency. Future work, if any, should avoid broad subgroup fishing and focus only on synthesis or a very small number of adequately powered, high-prior conditional checks with prespecified thresholds."
