  "narrative": "The four newly delivered comparisons are valid Welch analyses with at least 20 observations in every required cell, and all estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis directions. H301/R268 tests EGFR amplification with EGFR dependency among adenocarcinoma samples with screen_roc_auc at least 0.9555. The signed estimate is +0.018, interval -0.031 to +0.067. This is statistically indistinguishable from null and below the practical reference threshold for EGFR dependency near 0.025. The confidence interval is also wide enough that a clinically meaningful positive association in this selected stratum is not ruled out, so I assess this as unresolved rather than definitive absence, while closing the investigation because the independent-validation budget is exhausted and this is a narrow follow-up with repeated EGFR-related multiplicity. Conditioning on lineage and high screen quality creates a biologically and technically selected subset; it may reduce noise, but it also limits generalizability and could induce selection-related bias if screen quality is associated with both genomic annotation and dependency measurement.\n\nH302/R269 tests KRAS mutation with KRAS dependency among TP53-intact, STK11-intact cells. The signed estimate is +0.008, interval -0.014 to +0.030, with adequate cell sizes. The point estimate is very small relative to the KRAS-dependency practical threshold near 0.024 and the interval crosses zero. This is not evidence of a clinically meaningful KRAS-mutation effect in the tested genomic context. Given the earlier overall KRAS-dependency nulls and the consistency of small point estimates, I close this as unresolved rather than accept; the evidence is insufficient to support the hypothesis but also not precise enough to prove absence in this specific co-alteration subset.\n\nH303/R270 tests whether ARID1A co-loss adds to SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss cells. The signed estimate is +0.034, interval -0.013 to +0.082. The point estimate is below the SMARCA2 practical reference threshold near 0.042, the lower bound is negative, and the interval is wide. Thus the result does not support an additional ARID1A co-loss amplification effect in SMARCA4-deficient cells, but because the exposed cell count is modest and the upper interval exceeds the practical threshold, I treat this as unresolved rather than a definitive negative. This result does not weaken the accepted main SMARCA4-loss to SMARCA2-dependency association; it only fails to establish a reproducible incremental ARID1A effect in this dataset.\n\nH304/R271 tests CDKN2A loss with MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact cells that lack MYC amplification. The signed estimate is +0.116, interval +0.072 to +0.159, well above the practical reference threshold near 0.027 and statistically positive. This supports the accepted core CDKN2A/TP53-intact MDM2 association in a MYC-amplification-negative stratum and reduces concern that the core effect is carried mainly by MYC-amplified co-alteration patterns. It remains a dataset-only association; conditioning on MYC-negative status is a specificity test, not proof that MYC amplification is a causal confounder or that CDKN2A itself is the causal 9p21 locus.\n\nNo validation request is made because the independent-validation budget is exhausted and remaining_new_requests is zero. The main conclusion from this iteration is that the six core accepted dataset associations remain intact. H304 strengthens the CDKN2A/MDM2 finding in a MYC-negative context, while H301-H303 provide no support for additional clinically meaningful direct-driver or co-loss effects in the narrow contexts tested. These results should be interpreted as internal associations only, not as causal statements or external generalizability claims.",
  "research_notes": "Updated dataset-only conclusions after H301-H304. The six core accepted associations remain: SMARCA4 loss for stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high for stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss for weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss for stronger STAT3 dependency; TP53 loss for weaker USP7 dependency; and CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells for stronger MDM2 dependency. H304/R271 adds internal specificity support for the CDKN2A/TP53-intact MDM2 association in MYC-amplification-negative cells, with signed estimate +0.116 and interval +0.072 to +0.159. The four prespecified follow-ups show no new clinically meaningful direct-driver or incremental co-loss associations: H301 EGFR amplification in high-quality adenocarcinoma is unresolved/null-pointing (signed +0.018, -0.031 to +0.067); H302 KRAS mutation in TP53/STK11-intact cells is small and unresolved (signed +0.008, -0.014 to +0.030); H303 ARID1A co-loss within SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 is below threshold and inconclusive (signed +0.034, -0.013 to +0.082). Independent validation budget is exhausted. No further broad subgroup search is warranted. The best next scientific step remains prespecified external replication of exactly the six core signed associations with the same exposure definitions, eligibility, sign conventions, practical thresholds, and cell-count rules. Mechanistic follow-up would require orthogonal perturbation or rescue experiments, not additional observational subgroup contrasts."
